<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Danish West Indies</h1><p class="st-meta">1672 CE – 1917 CE · capital Charlotte Amalie</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Denmark's Caribbean sugar colony of St Thomas, St John and St Croix, built on slave labour and sold to the United States in 1917.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-west-india-and-guinea-company">Danish West India Company</a> settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-u-s-virgin-islands">St Thomas</a> in 1672 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-john-u-s-virgin-islands">St John</a> in 1718, and bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">St Croix</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1733. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-amalie">Charlotte Amalie</a> on St Thomas, with its excellent harbour, became the capital and a busy free port.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> plantations worked by enslaved Africans made the islands profitable; at their peak most of the population was enslaved. An uprising on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-john-u-s-virgin-islands">St John</a> in 1733 was crushed with French help. In 1848, facing a revolt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">St Croix</a>, Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-von-scholten">Peter von Scholten</a> declared emancipation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-amalie">Charlotte Amalie</a> thrived as a trading and coaling port where English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> were widely spoken.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> declined, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> sought to sell the islands. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, worried about German naval bases, bought them for 25 million dollars, and they were transferred in 1917, becoming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-virgin-islands">United States Virgin Islands</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/danish-west-indies.jpg" alt="Danish West Indies" width="320" height="242"><figcaption>Image: Madden and others · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Denmark.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1672 CE – 1917 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Charlotte Amalie</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Peter von Scholten</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran and Moravian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Danish, English, Negerhollands creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United States</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Danish Virgin Islands, Dansk Vestindien</dd></dl></aside></div>
